  "metric_definition_and_consistency_notes": {
    "binary_vs_three_class_relationship": "Because the held-out test set contains zero High-risk businesses (support = 0), High-risk F1 is 0.0 for all models. Consequently, the 3-class Macro-F1 is mathematically identical to (2/3) * Binary Macro-F1. For example, for the Selected Candidate: 0.5017 * (2/3) = 0.3344 exactly. Similarly, balanced_accuracy_score() averages recall across classes present in y_true (Low and Medium), making test balanced accuracy mathematically identical to binary balanced accuracy.",
    "confusion_matrix_consistency_audit": "In the previous conversational summary, the candidate confusion matrix was mistyped as [[110, 11, 0], [26, 3, 0], [0, 0, 0]], which falsely implied 113 correct predictions (75.33%) and predicted counts 136/14/0. The actual array on disk and in reports/component4/final_research_validation_report.json has always been [[111, 10, 0], [26, 3, 0], [0, 0, 0]], which yields exactly 111 + 3 = 114 correct predictions (114/150 = 76.00%) and predicted counts 137 / 13 / 0. Similarly, GNN v2.1 was mistyped as [[77, 39, 5], [16, 11, 2], [0, 0, 0]] (implying 93/50/7), whereas the real array on disk is [[76, 40, 5], [17, 11, 1], [0, 0, 0]], which yields exactly 87 correct predictions (58.00%) and predicted counts 93 / 51 / 6.",
    "high_risk_disclosure": "High-risk support in the held-out test cohort is 0. High-risk recall, precision, and F1 on unseen test data are empirically and statistically unevaluable. No claim of successful High-risk detection or default prediction on unseen businesses is supported by the data.",
    "gnn_vs_rf_significance_conclusion": "Paired bootstrap testing reveals that while the Focal Loss GNN achieves a statistically significant improvement in raw accuracy over RF (+20.70%, 95% CI [+10.65%, +31.33%]) by aligning with the majority Low-risk class (111/121), it does NOT achieve statistically significant superiority in Balanced Accuracy (Delta = -0.55%, 95% CI [-12.87%, +11.87%]) or Macro-F1 (Delta = +0.0148, 95% CI [-0.0628, +0.1010]). Random Forest achieved higher Medium-risk recall (44.8% vs 10.3%). Any claim of general GNN superiority over Random Forest is unsupported by the empirical evidence."
